{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789234089,"nonce":"EhAJZHfaU97+AQ8L1igisBzQz2U=","title":"A comparison takes its operand type and result type from the node, not the operands","message":"The Binary lowering typed a comparison's result as `convert_type(expr.ty)`\nand ran the instruction at `convert_type(left.ty)`. Both come from the\nfrontend's annotation of the node, which ZynML's parser leaves as Any\nfor an untyped `st \u003c 0`. While Any lowered to i64 this was invisible;\nwith Any a pointer type the compare became a pointer compare, unsigned,\nand `-1 \u003c 0` was false:\n\n    def f(n: i64, st: i64): i64 { mut hi = n; if st \u003c 0 { hi = 0 - 1 }; return hi }\n    f(6, -1)   // 6\n\nA comparison's value is Bool regardless of the node, and its instruction\nruns at the type the left operand actually has in HIR. The short-circuit\nmerge phi is likewise Bool unless the frontend declared a primitive.","files":null}]}